tested the harness** rather than trusting 17 green checks. The suite ran in ~0ms, which is exactly what a vacuously-passing harness looks like (e.g. one accidentally loading the C `.so` twice). Five injected mutants — a single-bit table change, a dropped `m__offset` term, `h >> 11`, mask `0x1ff`, and `wrapping_sub` — were each caught. I also confirmed the two libraries resolve to distinct files, and guarded the float-comparison traps: `+0.0 == -0.0` and `NaN != NaN` mean `==` would be silently vacuous, so all comparisons use raw `to_bits()`, and the NaN test fails if it never observes a NaN.\n\nOne process note: mid-run my `$TMPDIR` shifted between tool calls, so a backup vanished and four mutations **accumulated** in `src/lib.rs`. I caught it, reverted the three surviving edits, and re-confirmed the restored file against the C table-by-table (2048/64/64 entries, zero differences) before the final run. `c_src/` was never modified.\n\nDeliverables in `translation/`: `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md`, `tests/differential.rs` (17 tests), `tests/common/mod.rs`, and `run_all.sh` which builds both libraries, checks symbol parity, and sweeps every profile × feature combination. Note it uses `--offline`, since crates.io was unreachable through the proxy and `libloading` 0.8.9 was already in the local registry cache."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":278,"cache_read_input_tokens":99605,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":278,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b4de83a4-b33c-45b9-8ba9-88e3fc787451","uuid":"26e0d05a-f8cf-428a-80e8-8561bfda47fa","timestamp":"2026-09-05T00:44:21.144Z"}
